      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season lamb with salt. Heat oil in a large pot over high heat. Brown lamb on all sides for 5 minutes. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same pot, fry onion over medium heat for 8 minutes until golden. Add garlic and ginger, cook 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato puree and stir for 3 minutes. Pour in canned tomatoes. Cook uncovered over medium heat for 20 minutes until dark and the oil separates."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in peanut butter and 500ml water. Mix until smooth. Return lamb to the pot with chillies and bay leaves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add carrots and potatoes. Bring to a boil, reduce to a low simmer and cook covered for 45 minutes until lamb is tender."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste and adjust salt. Serve with rice or fufu."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Stew is grainy or split",
              "cause": "Peanut butter added to cold liquid or not stirred in",
              "fix": "Thin the peanut butter with a ladle of hot broth first, then stir the mixture into the pot."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Lamb still tough at 45 minutes",
              "cause": "Shoulder cuts vary in toughness",
              "fix": "Continue cooking on low heat. Lamb shoulder needs up to 90 minutes in some cases. Add a little water if it thickens too much."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Mafe is the everyday stew of Senegal and Mali, cooked in home kitchens rather than restaurants. It is considered a dish of generosity since peanuts and meat together signal abundance. The Malian version (tigadegena, meaning groundnut sauce) is often vegetarian with sweet potato and aubergine. French colonists adopted a version they called poulet au mafé, which spread across francophone West Africa."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
